A||||14||86SE||How in the heck do I remove the vibration dapener???||||8||
Z||000000||86SE||11-06-2001||07:41 PM||||I have tried and tried to take off the vibration dampener/harmonic balancer off the 3.4L engine and I just cant get it.  I first attacked it with a wheel puller and ended up pulling the outer ring off its seal (its toast).  I kept trying and trying, using a breaker bar for the added leverage.  Took the puller off because it wasent budging, looked inside the hole to see if I was ruining the crank threads and it was full of metal shavings.<P>I dont think I destroyed the threads, but this stupid thing just wont come off!!<P>Anyone else had problems like this?  Anyone with any input?<P>Thanks,<BR>Chuck<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://fierodude.hypermart.net/mysig.jpg">     <BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>V-6 w/rod knock Yellow Fiero<BR> -- 3.4L Conversion *Back in session!* --||||reg||8
Z||000001||fiero56||11-06-2001||07:56 PM||||my suggestion = go buy a puller||||reg||1
Z||000002||Kasdan||11-06-2001||07:58 PM||||He said he used a puller...||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||11-06-2001||08:21 PM||||get a bigger puller. may have to get a thread chaser to clean it up after too.<P>*tip* DO NOT TRY TO BANG IT ON OR OFF, you can mess up the crank big time.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Patrick86GT||11-06-2001||09:35 PM||||would a big stick of expolsive work?.....||||reg||1
Z||000005||watts||11-06-2001||09:35 PM||||And make sure the puller you are using has a good cone tip on it (the swivel ones, not just a conical tip on the shaft), and that it's got really fine threads.<P>I've used cheap ones that had coarse threads and they didn't work at all.  Picked up a GOOD quality unit with nice fine threads and a hardened shaft and it pop's 'em all off no problem.<BR><P>------------------<BR>87/88 V6 5spd Notchback Hybrid||||reg||1
Z||000006||ig88vsbobafett||11-06-2001||09:36 PM||||Well on my 3.8 you take off the starter and run a prybar into it and hold it then you should be able to get the bolt off (It takes 2 PPL (Unless you got 4 arms) hope this helps  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||11-06-2001||10:17 PM||||He may have used a puller, but if he is pulling the outer ring off, he is using an incorrect puller.||||reg||1
Z||000008||watts||11-07-2001||02:21 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fiero56:<BR><B>He may have used a puller, but if he is pulling the outer ring off, he is using an incorrect puller.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Good point - 86SE, you ARE using a proper balancer puller, and not a gear puller aren't you?!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||revin||11-07-2001||07:50 AM||||use a butain torch very carefully to heat up the area, thenuse the puller....did you try WD40?||||reg||1
Z||000010||fiero56||11-07-2001||09:36 AM||||I would not recommend putting a torch on the crank.<P>Buy a harmonic balancer puller, and take it off. It doesn't sound like the thing is seized on there. It sounds to me the job has been inappropriately approached with incorrect tools.<P>Part of rebuilding an engine is getting the correct tools. I have spent hundreds already on tools for my build, and I haven't even gotten started.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Carrolles||11-07-2001||01:44 PM||||I used the puller set I got from Harbour Freight for $7.99<BR> <A HREF="http://www.harborfreight.com/cpi/ctaf/Displayitem.taf?itemnumber=37714" TARGET=_blank>http://www.harborfreight.com/cpi/ctaf/Displayitem.taf?itemnumber=37714</A> <P>It worked great.  It helps to use an impact wrench on it.<P>Don't use a gear puller that has jaws that grab or hook onto the outer ring.  As you've discovered you will pull the balancer apart.||||reg||1
Z||000012||SKIDMARK||11-07-2001||01:55 PM||||Hey, that Harbor Frieght puller looks just like my $45 Snap-on (good thing I didn't have to pay for it). I just took mine off last week with this puller....worked great!<P>------------------<BR>White '85 4cyl coupe<BR>Black '86 v-6 SE<BR>'81 CB900F Super Sport||||reg||1
Z||000013||Voytek||11-07-2001||02:38 PM||||Would there be anything wrong with using a steering wheel puller?  I'm doing the same thing on my car and someone suggested using a steering wheel puller.  I have one of those at home but wouldn't want to damage anything.<BR>Thanks.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Carrolles||11-07-2001||04:44 PM||||The things to consider are:<P>The center rod pushing on the crank needs to have an end fitting that won't damage the internal threads in the center of the crank shaft.<P>The puller should have bolts that thread into the damper flange so you are pulling on the strong part.<P>A steering wheel puller may accomplish this but usually they are fairly light weight construction and may not be able to generate enough force to pull the damper off the crankshaft.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
